---
title: C and C++ interoperability
description: Create strict C projects or add C sources and ABI headers to an existing C++ project.
section: Guides
order: 22
updated: '2026-10-13'
---

C is a bundled provider alongside C++. New C projects offer C17, C23, and C11; C17 is the default. They generate a static core library, optional application, and CTest correctness check through CMake. The public header includes `extern "C"` guards for C++ consumers.

## First run in Neovim

[Load Rivet](/docs/start-here/) and install CMake, Ninja, and a C compiler supporting your chosen mode. In a parent directory you choose:

```sh
mkdir interop
cd interop
nvim
```

1. Run `:RivetNew`, choose **New choices** if offered, then select `c`, **Current folder**, and `interop`.
2. Choose Git and comment-header preferences, **Application**, and **C17**. Review and apply with `c`.
3. Open `app/main.c`. Run `:RivetTask run`, select Debug, and expect `42`.
4. Run `:RivetTask test`. CTest should report one passing test.
5. Run `:RivetFile`, select **c**, choose **C source**, and create `extra` in the core target. CMake registration updates with the `.c` source. Save changes, then Build or Test again.

To try quickfix, temporarily remove the semicolon from the statement in `main.c`, save, and Build. Use `:copen` to jump to the error, restore the semicolon, and `:RivetTask repeat`.

The optional CLI equivalent is:

```sh
rivet new interop --language c --standard 17
rivet test --root interop
rivet run --root interop
```

C mode is strict (`C_STANDARD_REQUIRED YES`, extensions disabled). The selected compiler still determines which features of that mode are implemented. CMake, presets, task output, quickfix, and File API executable discovery use the shared workflow.

## Mix C into a C++ project

Run `:RivetFile`, select **C++**, then **C source for mixed C/C++** or **C header with extern C guards**. You can also select **c** and choose a source, header, or pair for an existing C++ target. A C source enables C in the generated target registration and requests C17 independently of the C++ standard. Use the header to declare the C ABI and include it from C++.

```sh
rivet file interop --kind c_source --target simulation_core
rivet file interop --kind c_header --target simulation_core
```

These commands assume an existing target named `simulation_core`. Match the declaration and definition names, then build and test. No C++ module or standard-library import is inserted into C sources.
